    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "gotcha, so i should probably double check the connections and rot. i've heard stories about weird, unexpected failures where everything looks fine but then something just snaps or breaks for no apparent reason. is there any kind of specific \"hidden\" cause or small error that tends to trigger those kinds of sudden collapses?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A surprisingly common hidden culprit is improper fastener size or spacing in connections, leading to joint failure before the main wood member breaks. Another subtle issue is moisture trapping beneath composite or decking materials, which accelerates localized wood decay in critical areas. Furthermore, improperly graded lumber, showing internal defects like knots or checks, can fail under load even if it looks sound superficially. Always ensure your connection hardware meets the specific load requirements for your structure.",
      "source": "target",
